    "project": "We recommend consolidating our three cloud vendors into one five-year contract from Q1 2027. The chosen vendor offers a 22% discount in year one, full migration support and a single point of contact. Savings are estimated at EUR 1.4M over five years.",
    "context": "Chief Financial Officer, board decision next month.",
    "report": "## Record\n- Triage: CRITICAL · examined: question, answer, reasoning, perspective, applicability\n- Findings: 3, by impact CRITICAL: 1 · HIGH: 2 · MODERATE: 0 · LOW: 0\n- Assumptions: 4 with a named verifier · 0 without\n- Questions: 5 with an owner · 1 without\n- Most useful question: \"What does leaving the contract cost if the price rises after the first year?\"\n- Independence: not stated by the user, to be confirmed by the signer\n\n## 1. Context and triage\nThis project is read as a procurement and finance recommendation for a Chief Financial Officer, intended to inform a five-year vendor commitment at board level. Triage: CRITICAL, because a single five-year contract is hard to undo and the financial commitment is high for the organisation. Examined: question, answer, reasoning, perspective, applicability.\nCriteria: reversibility HIGH · impact scope MODERATE · financial commitment HIGH → two criteria at HIGH or above → CRITICAL.\n\nThe five levels of examination: question (what was actually asked) · answer (what the project says) · reasoning (how it gets there) · perspective (what it did not see) · applicability (what it looks like in the real world). Triage: the project's stakes, LOW, MODERATE, HIGH or CRITICAL, judged on reversibility, impact scope and financial commitment; it sets which levels are examined.\n\nIf this framing does not match your situation, say so, and the triage will be redone.\n\n## 2. What holds up\nThe recommendation names one vendor, one start date and one contract length: each can be checked against the offers. A review of what stands, not an endorsement of its content.\n\n## 3. What deserves scrutiny\n\nNote on reasoning: an AI model does not reason in the human sense; the reasoning checks below test logical consistency, not the process that produced the project.\n\n**Pre-mortem: three plausible causes of failure · Impact: CRITICAL**\nObserved: the memo gives no scenario in which the consolidation fails.\nEffect on the decision: three plausible causes are left unexamined: prices rise after year one, a workload cannot move in time, the single vendor's service degrades with no way out.\nTo check: for each cause, what the contract says and who in the organisation would see it coming.\n\n**Exit cost not quantified · Impact: HIGH**\nObserved: the memo proposes a single five-year contract without quantifying what leaving it would cost.\nEffect on the decision: if the price rises after the first year, that cost weighs on the board's decision.\nTo check: ask procurement for the exit cost of each offer, year by year.\n\n**Prices for years 2 to 5 not given · Impact: HIGH**\nObserved: \"a 22% discount in year one\" supports \"savings are estimated at EUR 1.4M over five years\".\nEffect on the decision: a discount limited to year one says nothing about years two to five, where most of the contract value lies.\nTo check: the price schedule for every year of the contract, in the draft contract itself.\n\n## 4. Assumptions the project relies on\n1. Prices after year one stay close to year one. If they rise, the EUR 1.4M saving does not hold. → Who can verify: procurement, with the full price schedule.\n2. All workloads can move before the current contracts end. If not, two vendors are paid at the same time. → Who can verify: the CIO.\n3. The vendor's migration support covers the work the teams would otherwise do. If not, the internal cost is missing from the saving. → Who can verify: the CIO, with the heads of the teams concerned.\n4. One point of contact is enough for three former vendors' services. If not, service levels drop during the first year. → Who can verify: the head of IT operations.\n\n## 5. Questions before you decide\n1. What does leaving the contract cost if the price rises after the first year? Owner: procurement\n2. What are the prices for years 2 to 5 of each offer? Owner: procurement\n3. Which workloads cannot move before the current contracts end? Owner: CIO\n4. What internal effort does the migration need, beyond the vendor's support? Owner: CIO\n5. Which service levels does the contract guarantee, and what happens if they are missed? Owner: head of IT operations\n6. Who will answer to the board if the saving is not there in year three?\n\nNo verdict. The decision is yours."
